{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_admin","statement":"A mismatched cookie domain causes the admin login page to reappear with no error; fix by setting SESSION_COOKIE_DOMAIN to match the browser's domain, e.g. 'www.example.com'.","source_span":"Try setting the :setting:`SESSION_COOKIE_DOMAIN` setting to match your domain... set ``SESSION_COOKIE_DOMAIN = 'www.example.com'``."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_admin","statement":"The admin site only allows access to users with both is_active and is_staff set to True.","source_span":"The admin site only allows access to users with those two fields both set to True."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_admin","statement":"Customizing the ModelAdmin save_model hook lets you update an object to reflect the user who last edited it, by extracting the current user from the request.","source_span":"customizing the :meth:`~django.contrib.admin.ModelAdmin.save_model` hook, you can update an object to reflect the user that edited it"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_admin","statement":"The ModelAdmin get_queryset and has_change_permission methods can be used to limit admin visibility and editability to only the users who created an object.","source_span":"the :meth:`~django.contrib.admin.ModelAdmin.get_queryset` and :meth:`~django.contrib.admin.ModelAdmin.has_change_permission` can be used to control the visibility and editability of objects in the admin"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_admin","statement":"Django won't display a list_filter for a ManyToManyField if there are no related objects in the database.","source_span":"Django won't bother displaying the filter for a ``ManyToManyField`` if there are no related objects."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_admin","statement":"A record with a ForeignKey pointing to a nonexistent object, when that FK is in list_display, will not be shown in the admin changelist because of an integrity constraint not enforced at the database level.","source_span":"the record will not be shown in the admin changelist because the Django model is declaring an integrity constraint that is not implemented at the database level"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_admin","statement":"Custom JavaScript modules can be attached to the admin add/change form via the model's class Admin js parameter, a list of URLs included via a <script> tag.","source_span":"attach arbitrary JavaScript modules to the page via the model's class Admin :ref:`js parameter... included within the admin form via a ``<script>`` tag"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_admin","statement":"On desktop the admin supports recent versions of Chrome, Edge, Firefox, Opera, and Safari; on mobile/tablet it supports major browsers on Android and iOS.","source_span":"this means Chrome, Edge, Firefox, Opera, Safari, and others... This includes the major browsers on both Android and iOS."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_admin","statement":"The admin's assistive technology support target includes Dragon, JAWS, NVDA, Orca, TalkBack, Voice Control, VoiceOver iOS, VoiceOver macOS, Windows Contrast Themes, ZoomText, and screen magnifiers.","source_span":"including Dragon, JAWS, NVDA, Orca, TalkBack, Voice Control, VoiceOver iOS, VoiceOver macOS, Windows Contrast Themes, ZoomText, and screen magnifiers"}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_django_admin","fact_refs":[0],"question":"I enter a valid username and password on the Django admin login page but it just redisplays the login form with no error message. What's the likely cause and fix?","answer":"The login cookie's domain doesn't match your browser's domain. Set SESSION_COOKIE_DOMAIN to match your domain, e.g. SESSION_COOKIE_DOMAIN = 'www.example.com' if you're accessing https://www.example.com/admin/."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_django_admin","fact_refs":[1],"question":"Which two User model fields must both be True for a user to access the Django admin?","answer":"is_active and is_staff must both be set to True."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_django_admin","fact_refs":[2],"question":"Which ModelAdmin hook should be customized to automatically record the user who last edited an object?","answer":"The save_model hook, by extracting the current user from the request."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_django_admin","fact_refs":[3],"question":"Which two ModelAdmin methods restrict admin objects so they're only visible/editable by the users who created them?","answer":"get_queryset and has_change_permission."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_django_admin","fact_refs":[4],"question":"Why might a ManyToManyField not appear as a filter in list_filter?","answer":"Django won't display a filter for a ManyToManyField if there are no related objects in the database, since such filtering would be meaningless."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_django_admin","fact_refs":[5],"question":"Why might a database record not appear in the Django admin changelist even though it exists?","answer":"This happens when the record has a ForeignKey pointing to a nonexistent object and that foreign key is included in list_display — the model declares an integrity constraint that isn't enforced at the database level, so the record is excluded."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_contributing","statement":"There is a dedicated document titled \"Contributing to Django\" for getting started contributing code.","source_span":"It's titled :doc:`Contributing to Django </internals/contributing/index>`."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_contributing","statement":"Django's developers are volunteers, so the time available to work on the framework varies week to week.","source_span":"the people who work on Django are all volunteers. As a result, the amount of time that we have to work on the framework is limited and will vary from week to week"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_contributing","statement":"Three things help a ticket avoid getting stuck: clear reproduction instructions, clarity about which of several linked branches matter, and inclusion of a unit test.","source_span":"Are there clear instructions on how to reproduce the bug?... is it clear what each one does... Does the change include a unit test?"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_contributing","statement":"A contribution unsuitable for inclusion in Django will have its ticket closed rather than be ignored.","source_span":"If your contribution is not suitable for inclusion in Django, we won't ignore it -- we'll close the ticket."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_contributing","statement":"Contributors can post gentle reminders in the #contributing-getting-started channel on the Django Discord server.","source_span":"Gentle reminders in the ``#contributing-getting-started`` channel in the `Django Discord server`_ can work."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_contributing","statement":"Posting a reminder right before a release deadline is unlikely to get the attention a contributor wants.","source_span":"If you post your message right before a release deadline, you're not likely to get the sort of attention you require."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_contributing","statement":"Emailing personally or repeatedly raising the same issue will not gain a contributor additional attention.","source_span":"Please refrain from emailing anyone personally or repeatedly raising the same issue over and over again."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_contributing","statement":"One prioritization criterion for bug fixes is the number of people likely affected; bugs affecting many people get priority over edge cases.","source_span":"One of the criteria that is used to prioritize bug fixes is the number of people that will likely be affected by a given bug."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_contributing","statement":"A contributor cannot mark their own ticket as \"Ready For Checkin\" — another set of eyes is needed first.","source_span":"Sorry, no. It's always better to get another set of eyes on a ticket."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_contributing","statement":"When balancing limited time, the Django team errs toward making 10 people happy rather than a single person happy.","source_span":"we will always err on the side of making 10 people happy rather than making a single person happy"}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_django_contributing","fact_refs":[2],"question":"What three things help ensure a submitted Django bug-fix ticket doesn't stall before checkin?","answer":"Clear instructions for reproducing the bug (clear even to someone unfamiliar with the relevant dependency, contrib module, or database), clarity about which of several linked branches matter, and inclusion of a unit test demonstrating the fix."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_django_contributing","fact_refs":[4],"question":"Where can a Django contributor post a gentle reminder about a ticket they care about?","answer":"In the #contributing-getting-started channel on the Django Discord server."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_django_contributing","fact_refs":[5],"question":"Why is it a bad idea to remind the Django team about your ticket right before a release deadline?","answer":"You're not likely to get the attention you need at that time, since the team is focused on the release."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_django_contributing","fact_refs":[7],"question":"Can a Django contributor mark their own ticket as \"Ready For Checkin\"?","answer":"No — it's always better to get another set of eyes on a ticket first."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_django_contributing","fact_refs":[3],"question":"What happens to a Django contribution that the team decides isn't suitable for inclusion?","answer":"The ticket gets closed rather than left open and ignored."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_django_contributing","fact_refs":[6],"question":"What is one criterion the Django team uses to prioritize which bugs get fixed first?","answer":"The number of people likely to be affected by the bug — bugs affecting many people take priority over edge cases affecting few."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_general","statement":"Django grew from World Online's need, as a newspaper web operation, to build intensive web applications under journalism deadlines.","source_span":"Django grew from a very practical need: World Online, a newspaper web operation, is responsible for building intensive web applications on journalism deadlines."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_general","statement":"In fall 2003, World Online developers Adrian Holovaty and Simon Willison switched from PHP to Python to develop websites.","source_span":"In fall 2003, the World Online developers (Adrian Holovaty and Simon Willison) ditched PHP and began using Python to develop its websites."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_general","statement":"World Online decided to open-source Django in summer 2005.","source_span":"In summer 2005, World Online decided to open-source the resulting software, Django."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_general","statement":"Django is named after Django Reinhardt, a jazz manouche guitarist active from the 1930s to early 1950s.","source_span":"Django is named after `Django Reinhardt`_, a jazz manouche guitarist from the 1930s to early 1950s."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_general","statement":"Django is pronounced \"JANG-oh\" (rhymes with FANG-oh), with a silent \"D\".","source_span":"Django is pronounced **JANG**-oh. Rhymes with FANG-oh. The \"D\" is silent."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_general","statement":"Companies including Disqus, Instagram, Pinterest, and Mozilla have used Django for many years.","source_span":"Companies like Disqus, Instagram, Pinterest, and Mozilla have been using Django for many years."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_general","statement":"Sites built on Django have weathered traffic spikes of over 50 thousand hits per second.","source_span":"Sites built on Django have weathered traffic spikes of over 50 thousand hits per second."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_general","statement":"Django uses a \"shared-nothing\" architecture, allowing hardware to be added at any level: database, caching, or web/application servers.","source_span":"Django uses a \"shared-nothing\" architecture, which means you can add hardware at any level -- database servers, caching servers or web/application servers."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_general","statement":"Django was originally developed at World Online, the web department of a newspaper in Lawrence, Kansas, USA.","source_span":"Django was originally developed at World Online, the web department of a newspaper in Lawrence, Kansas, USA."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_general","statement":"Django is distributed under the 3-clause BSD license.","source_span":"Django is distributed under :source:`the 3-clause BSD license <LICENSE>`."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_general","statement":"Django includes a copy of the Python license because Django includes code from the Python standard library.","source_span":"Django includes code from the Python standard library... A copy of the Python license... is included with Django for compliance with Python's terms."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_general","statement":"In Django's MTV interpretation, \"view\" is the Python callback function describing which data is presented, and the framework itself effectively plays the role of \"controller\".","source_span":"a \"view\" is the Python callback function for a particular URL, because that callback function describes which data is presented... In Django's case, it's probably the framework itself: the machinery that sends a request to the appropriate view"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_general","statement":"Django is not a content-management-system (CMS).","source_span":"No, Django is not a CMS, or any sort of \"turn"}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_django_general","fact_refs":[0],"question":"Why was Django originally created?","answer":"It grew out of World Online's practical need, as a newspaper web operation, to build intensive web applications quickly under journalism deadlines."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_django_general","fact_refs":[1],"question":"Who created Django, and when did they switch from PHP to Python?","answer":"World Online developers Adrian Holovaty and Simon Willison switched from PHP to Python in fall 2003."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_django_general","fact_refs":[2],"question":"When did World Online open-source Django?","answer":"In summer 2005."}
